  "coaching.play-mode": "Play mode: I stay quiet unless you ask. Ask me for a hint, an analysis or a review at any time.",
  "commands.list": "**How the chat box works**\nType in the box at the bottom and press Send (or Enter). I understand short requests in plain English, a point on the board such as \"D4\", or a letter from my suggestions such as \"A\". I am not an AI model, so short, simple requests work best. If I do not understand, try the words shown below.\n\n**During your game**\n\"hint\": I suggest a good move and ring it on the board.\n\"why\": the idea behind the last move I explained.\n\"analyse\": my five best moves, marked A to E, with their chances of winning.\n\"why A?\" or \"compare A and B\": what one suggestion does, or why one is better.\n\"what about D4?\": what a move at D4 would do.\n\"play D4\": play a move without tapping the board.\n\"am I in danger?\": groups that are short of liberties.\n\"explain the position\": who has which part of the board, and who is ahead.\n\"review\": how each side played, move by move.\n\"undo\", \"pass\", \"resign\", \"new game\".\n\n**Looking ahead**\n\"show me\": I pick the move I would play and follow it on a copy of the board. You step through it with \"next\" and \"back\" (or ← Back and Next → under the board), and can ask \"why?\" or other questions along the way.\n\"play both sides\": on the other side's turn I mark my five best moves and you choose one, or play any other point. \"computer plays\" switches it off. You can also choose it in the New game box.\n\"explore D4\" or \"explore A\": follow a move a few steps ahead on a copy of the board. Then \"next\", \"back\", \"another\" (a different reply) and \"back to game\".\n\n**Learning**\n\"teach me\", \"lessons\" or \"lesson 3\": short lessons with a task each.\n\"basic rules\": step-by-step teaching on a 9×9 board.\n\"guided game\": a first game with the coach explaining each move.\n\"set up the board\": place and remove stones by hand (also the Set up button), then play on from that position.\n\"what is ko?\" or \"what does kifu mean?\": the meaning of a Go term (also liberty, atari, eye, joseki, kyu and dan, hane, tsumego, moyo, ponnuki, tiger's mouth and many more).\n\"Go terms\" or \"glossary\": the list of all the Go terms I can explain.\n\"rules\", \"first move\", \"checklist\": the rules in brief, where to start, and five questions to ask before every move.\n\"famous games\" and \"history of Go\".\n\n**Settings**\n\"show liberties\" or \"hide liberties\", \"show coordinates\" or \"hide coordinates\", \"turn on sound\" or \"turn off sound\".\n\nType \"help\" to see the welcome again, or \"commands\" to see this list.",
  "commentary.idea.capture": "Idea: a captured stone is gone for good. Before each move, ask: can I capture something safely?",
  "commentary.idea.rescue": "Idea: check for atari before every move. A group with one liberty can vanish in a single move.",
  "commentary.idea.atari": "Idea: atari is a threat, not a capture. It works best when the group cannot escape, or when the reply it forces helps you.",
  "commentary.idea.connect": "Idea: connected stones share their liberties. Stones left apart can be attacked one at a time.",
  "commentary.idea.cut": "Idea: cutting turns one strong group into two weak ones, and weak groups have to spend moves defending.",
  "commentary.idea.territory": "Idea: your score is your stones plus the empty points you surround, so claiming big empty areas early is efficient.",
  "commentary.idea.approach": "Idea: a stone placed near the opponent’s stone limits how much space that stone can claim.",
  "commentary.idea.build": "Idea: stones that support each other make a position strong, and a strong position makes territory easier to hold.",
  "commentary.idea.boundary": "Idea: where two sides meet, each move decides how far each side’s area reaches. Moves on the boundary are often the biggest on the board.",
  "commentary.idea.line": "Idea: the third line is the \"line of territory\" (safe and good for points) and the fourth is the \"line of influence\" (more power towards the centre).",
  "commentary.checklist": "Five questions to ask before every move:\n1. Is any group in atari, mine or my opponent’s?\n2. Can I capture something, or must I rescue something?\n3. Are my groups connected, or can the opponent cut between them?\n4. Where is the biggest empty area, and can I claim it?\n5. Will my new stone have enough liberties to be safe?\nAnswer them in that order: danger first, then opportunity, then territory.",
