  "guide.def.coord": "Every point has a name, like a map reference: the letter is the column and the number is the row.",
  "guide.def.liberty": "A liberty is an empty point right next to a stone: above, below, left or right (never diagonal). Liberties are what keep a stone alive.",
  "guide.def.capture": "A stone or group with no liberties left is captured: it is taken off the board and kept as a prisoner.",
  "guide.def.atari": "Atari means a group has only one liberty left, so it will be captured on the next move unless it escapes.",
  "guide.def.territory": "Territory means the empty points that only your stones surround. At the end of the game your score is your stones plus your territory.",
  "guide.def.edge": "Stones on the very edge of the board (the first line) have few liberties and surround almost nothing, so they are usually weak early in the game.",
  "guide.def.line": "This point is on the third or fourth line from the edge, which is a good balance: close enough to the edge to be safe, far enough to claim space.",
  "guide.tip-1": "Tip: stones are stronger when they are connected, touching along a line. A connected group shares its liberties, so it is harder to capture.",
  "guide.tip-2": "Tip: corners and sides need fewer stones to surround an area than the middle does. That is why most games start near the corners.",
  "guide.tip-3": "Tip: before every move, look at each of your groups and count its liberties. If a group has only one, save it first.",
  "guide.tip-4": "Tip: you do not have to capture anything to win. Surrounding more empty points than White is enough.",
  "guide.tip-5": "Tip: if you are not sure where to play, type \"hint\". You can also ask \"what is a liberty?\", or type \"undo\" to take a move back.",
