  "guide.title": "**Your first game**",
  "guide.intro": "This is a real game of Go, played gently. You are {you} and the computer plays {cpu} at its easiest level. {first}",
  "guide.intro-handicap": "This is a real game of Go, played gently. You are {you} and the computer plays {cpu} at its easiest level. Black starts with {count} stones already on the board (the handicap stones, on the star points). In Go, a stronger player gives the weaker player a head start so that the game is fair. {first}",
  "guide.white-first": "White moves first.",
  "guide.you-first": "You move first.",
  "guide.how": "**How to play**\n1. Tap an empty point, a place where two lines cross, to put a stone there. On a phone, tap once to preview the stone and again to confirm. Stones never move once placed.\n2. Your aim is to surround more empty points than your opponent (this is called territory), and to capture enemy stones that run out of liberties.\n3. After every move I will explain in plain words what happened and what to look for next.\n4. You can ask me anything at any time, for example \"what is a liberty?\", or type \"hint\" for a suggested move, or \"undo\" to take a move back.\n5. When you cannot find a useful move, press Pass. When both players pass, the game ends and we count the points.",
  "guide.your-move": "**Your move: {point}**",
  "guide.placed": "You placed a {colour} stone at {point}.",
  "guide.captured": "You captured {stones}!",
  "guide.prisoners": "They are now your prisoners.",
  "guide.group-libs": "Your group now has {libs}.",
  "guide.stone-libs": "Your stone has {libs}.",
  "guide.self-atari-group": "Careful: that leaves the group with a single liberty, at {point}. That is atari, and White could capture it by playing there.",
  "guide.self-atari-stone": "Careful: that leaves the stone with a single liberty, at {point}. That is atari, and White could capture it by playing there.",
  "guide.enemy-atari": "{player}'s {what} is now in atari: it has only one liberty left, at {point}. If White does not rescue it, you can capture it by playing at {point}.",
  "guide.cpu-move": "**{player}'s move: {point}**",
  "guide.cpu-placed": "The computer ({player}) placed a stone at {point}.",
  "guide.cpu-captured": "It captured {stones} of yours.",
  "guide.dont-worry": "Do not worry: losing a stone is part of learning. Look at how it happened, so you can avoid it next time.",
  "guide.touches": "It touches {list}, which takes away one of {their} liberties.",
  "guide.your-turn": "**Your turn**",
  "guide.warning": "Warning: {what} is in atari. It has only one liberty, at {point}, so White will capture it next move unless you rescue it.",
  "guide.rescue-how": "To rescue it, play at {point} yourself: that adds a stone to the group and gives it new liberties. The other way is to capture one of the white stones that are attacking it.",
  "guide.chance": "Chance: {what} (White) is in atari. If you play at {point}, you capture it.",
  "guide.how-first": "Tap an empty point to place your stone. Not sure where? Type \"hint\".",
  "guide.how-again": "Look for a good empty point, or type \"hint\".",
  "guide.you-passed": "**You passed**\nPassing means skipping your turn because you think there is nothing useful left to play. If White passes straight after, the game ends and we count.",
  "guide.white-passes": "**White passes**\nThe computer thinks nothing useful is left to play. If you also pass now, the game ends. If you can still gain something, keep playing.",
  "guide.over": "**The game is over**\nBoth players passed in a row, so we count. I have marked the stones I think are dead (stones that could not survive); they are taken off the board before counting. Tap a group to change my mind.\nEach side scores its stones plus its territory. White also adds komi ({komi} points), a bonus for moving second, so a tie is impossible. Press Confirm result when you agree with the marks.",
